from core import L
L(3,'c-memory','C types, expressions, pointers and memory','Programming in C;Pointers;Arrays;Storage;Expressions','Basic binary arithmetic and functions.',
'C exposes the relationship between a value and its storage. A pointer stores an address; dereferencing accesses the object there. Tracing code requires tracking types, lifetimes and sequencing, rather than assuming every expression has a predictable machine result.',
'''Types and evaluation::Integer promotions and conversions affect arithmetic. Integer division truncates toward zero; floating operands change division behaviour. Logical AND and logical OR short-circuit, evaluating the right side only when necessary. sizeof measures bytes and normally does not evaluate its operand, except relevant variable-length-array cases. Do not assume int is always four bytes unless the question states the machine model.
||Pointers and arrays::For a pointer p to T, p+1 advances by sizeof(T) bytes within a valid array range. An array expression usually decays to a pointer to its first element, but sizeof on an actual in-scope array returns full array size. Function parameters written as arrays are adjusted to pointers. Dereferencing a one-past-the-end pointer is invalid even though forming it is permitted.
||Lifetime and undefined behaviour::Automatic local objects cease to exist after their scope exits; returning their addresses creates dangling pointers. Dynamically allocated storage survives until freed and must be checked for allocation failure. Out-of-bounds accesses and unsequenced conflicting modifications can be undefined behaviour. For such programs, reporting a single numerical output is not a valid analysis.''',
'''Trace an array with sizeof(int)=4::For int a[5], sizeof(a)=5×4=20 bytes while a remains an array in scope.~If p=&a[1], p+2 points to a[3], advancing 8 bytes.~Inside a function f(int a[]), sizeof(a) is pointer size, not 20.
||Evaluate 7/2 versus 7/2.0::7 and 2 are integer operands, so 7/2 gives 3.~2.0 is floating; 7 is converted and division gives 3.5.~Assigning the first expression to double afterward produces 3.0; conversion does not restore the discarded fraction.''',
'Address arithmetic scales by pointed-to type size.||Do not compute outputs for undefined behaviour.||Assignment target type does not retroactively change integer division.',
'sizeof(array)=element count×element size in array scope.||C arguments are passed by value; a copied pointer can still mutate its pointee.',
[('If sizeof(int)=4, how many bytes are in an actual int a[8] array?',32,'Multiply count by size.','8×4=32 bytes.'),('In C, what is the value of 9/4 when both operands are int?',2,'Integer division truncates toward zero.','9/4 is 2, with remainder 1.')])
L(3,'recursion','Recursion and stack-frame tracing','Recursion;Call stack;Recursive complexity','C functions; parameters; local variables.',
'A recursive call is another invocation with its own local state. You can reason inductively about the returned value or mechanically trace stack frames. Both require knowing where recursion stops and how an argument gets closer to that base case.',
'''Call and return model::Each call creates a frame with parameters, locals and return information. A recursive call suspends its caller until it returns. For factorial, fact(n) waits for fact(n−1), then multiplies. Statements before recursion run on descent; statements after recursion run during unwinding. Shared global state differs from per-frame locals.
||Correctness and termination::State a base case and a decreasing measure bounded below, such as remaining input length. An induction proof assumes smaller calls work and verifies the current combination. Recursion over graph edges needs a visited set when cycles are possible; merely calling neighbours is not guaranteed to terminate.
||Time versus space::Count the total calls for time and maximum simultaneous active frames for stack space. Factorial takes Θ(n) time and Θ(n) stack. A balanced divide recursion can take Θ(n) total work but Θ(log n) stack depth. Naive Fibonacci revisits subproblems exponentially; memoization stores repeated results and reduces time to Θ(n).''',
'''Trace f(n): if n=0 return; print n; f(n−1); print n, starting at 2::Call f(2) prints 2, then calls f(1), which prints 1.~f(0) immediately returns; f(1) then prints 1.~f(2) resumes and prints 2, giving 2,1,1,2.
||Count calls in factorial(4) with base factorial(0)::The active call chain is 4→3→2→1→0.~There are 5 calls and maximum stack depth 5 under this model.~Unwinding returns 1,1,2,6,24. Do not count only the 4 multiplications as the number of calls.''',
'Count base-case invocations too.||Total call count and stack depth need not have the same asymptotic size.',
'Recursion needs a base case and progress.||Time counts work; stack space counts the deepest active chain.',
[('Factorial(5) recurses down to factorial(0). How many calls occur?',6,'Include zero.','Calls are 5,4,3,2,1,0: six invocations.'),('A recurrence T(n)=T(n−1)+1 has T(0)=1. What is T(8)?',9,'One unit is added for each level.','T(n)=n+1, so T(8)=9.')])
L(3,'linear-structures','Arrays, linked lists, stacks and queues','Arrays;Linked lists;Stacks;Queues;Circular queue','Pointers and array indexing.',
'Data structures trade access speed against update cost. A complexity statement must specify what information you already possess: inserting after a known linked-list node is cheap, but finding that node may dominate the operation.',
'''Arrays and linked lists::Contiguous arrays allow constant-time indexed access because address=base+index×element size. Middle insertion shifts a suffix, taking Θ(n). A singly linked list stores a next pointer and supports O(1) insertion after a known node. Finding a predecessor or an indexed element takes O(n). Doubly linked lists store previous links too, enabling deletion of a known node without a predecessor search.
||Stacks and queues::Stacks remove the most recently inserted item and support recursion, expression evaluation and DFS. Queues remove the oldest and support BFS. A linked queue can enqueue at the tail and dequeue at the head in O(1) with both pointers. Reversing these ends in a singly linked list makes tail deletion expensive.
||Circular arrays::Advance queue indices modulo capacity. Empty and full can both appear as head=tail unless you track a count, a full flag, or leave one slot unused. A capacity-n array with an unused-slot convention holds only n−1 elements. Carefully check whether an index denotes the next free location or the last occupied location.''',
'''Evaluate postfix “2 3 + 4 *”::Push 2 then 3; + pops both and pushes 5.~Push 4; * pops 4 and 5 and pushes 20.~The final stack contains 20. For subtraction preserve left/right operand order when popping.
||A queue has capacity 5 and next-free tail index 4. Enqueue twice::The first insertion uses slot 4 and advances tail to (4+1)%5=0.~The second uses slot 0 and advances tail to 1.~The wrap is arithmetic modulo capacity; valid insertion also requires checking available space.''',
'List insertion is not O(1) if the position first needs a search.||Circular queue capacity depends on the chosen full/empty convention.',
'Stack=LIFO; queue=FIFO.||Array access O(1), middle insertion Θ(n).',
[('Push 4,7,9 on an empty stack and pop once. What value is returned?',9,'Take the most recent push.','LIFO returns 9.'),('A queue tail index is 7 in a capacity-8 ring. What index follows one increment?',0,'Use modulo 8.','(7+1)%8=0.')])
L(3,'trees-bst','Trees, traversals and binary search trees','Trees;Binary search trees;Tree traversals','Recursion and pointer-based structures.',
'A tree is a connected acyclic structure. Rooting it introduces parents, children and subtrees. Binary search trees add an ordering invariant, so searching can discard one entire subtree at each comparison—but only balanced height guarantees logarithmic depth.',
'''Tree terminology and traversals::Height in edges is the longest root-to-leaf edge count; some problems instead count nodes, so read the convention. Preorder visits root,left,right; inorder left,root,right; postorder left,right,root. A binary tree has at most two children per node. A full binary tree gives each internal node exactly two children; a complete tree fills levels left to right.
||BST operations::For distinct keys, everything in a node’s left subtree is smaller and everything in the right is larger. Search follows one comparison-directed path. Insertion finds a null child location. Deletion handles no child, one child, or two children; the two-child case replaces the key with inorder successor or predecessor and deletes that replacement node.
||Complexity and reconstruction::Search, insertion and deletion take O(h). Sorted insertion into an unbalanced BST can create h=n−1. Balanced trees maintain logarithmic height. Inorder traversal produces sorted keys, not insertion order. With distinct keys, inorder plus preorder or postorder uniquely determine a binary tree; preorder alone generally does not determine an arbitrary binary tree.''',
'''Insert 4,2,6,1,3 into a BST::4 becomes root; 2 goes left; 6 goes right.~1 goes left of 2 and 3 right of 2.~Inorder is 1,2,3,4,6; preorder is 4,2,1,3,6. Trace the visit rule rather than memorizing a shape.
||Delete root 4 from that tree using its successor::The smallest key in the right subtree is 6.~Replace 4 by 6 and delete the old leaf containing 6.~The remaining root 6 has the original left subtree and no right child; the BST invariant remains true.''',
'Complete, full and perfect trees are different definitions.||A BST is not automatically balanced.',
'BST inorder is sorted.||BST operations take O(height).||Perfect binary tree nodes=2^(h+1)−1 for edge-height h.',
[('A perfect binary tree has edge-height 3. How many nodes does it contain?',15,'Use the geometric level sum.','1+2+4+8=15.'),('Insert 1,2,3,4 into an unbalanced BST. What is its edge-height?',3,'Ascending keys create a right chain.','Four nodes on one path contain 3 edges.')])
L(3,'heaps-graphs','Binary heaps and graph representations','Binary heaps;Graphs;Adjacency list;Adjacency matrix','Arrays, trees and graph basics.',
'A heap guarantees an extreme value at the root without sorting the entire collection. Graph representations instead organize many-to-many links. In both cases, choosing a representation determines which operations are cheap and which information must be searched.',
'''Heap invariants::A binary min-heap is a complete binary tree whose parent key is no larger than its child keys. In a zero-based array, left child=2i+1, right child=2i+2 and parent=floor((i−1)/2). Completeness gives height Θ(log n). Sibling order is unrestricted and a heap is not a BST.
||Heap updates and construction::Insertion appends and bubbles upward; delete-min swaps the final element to the root and sifts downward. Each traverses at most the height. Bottom-up construction sifts internal nodes from the last parent backward. It is Θ(n), since many nodes have tiny subtrees; multiplying n by the maximum sift cost overestimates aggregate work.
||Graph storage::An adjacency matrix uses Θ(V²) space and permits O(1) edge tests, but listing neighbours takes Θ(V). Adjacency lists use Θ(V+E) space and enumerate neighbours proportional to degree. An undirected graph usually stores each edge twice in lists. Weighted graphs attach weights; directed graphs distinguish outgoing from incoming edges.''',
'''Insert 2 into min-heap array [3,5,4]::Append to get [3,5,4,2].~Parent of index 3 is 1; swap 2 with 5 to obtain [3,2,4,5].~Parent of index 1 is 0; swap 2 with 3 to obtain [2,3,4,5]. Heap order is restored.
||Compare storage for V=1000,E=2000::A full adjacency matrix allocates 1000000 cells.~Adjacency-list edge entries are 2000 directed or 4000 undirected, plus vertex headers.~Sparse connectivity strongly favours list space, though a matrix provides constant-time edge membership tests.''',
'Heap order does not sort siblings or support BST-style search.||Array child formulas change for one-based indexing.',
'Heap insert/delete Θ(log n), bottom-up build Θ(n).||List space Θ(V+E), matrix space Θ(V²).',
[('In a zero-based heap, what is the right-child index of node 4?',10,'Use 2i+2.','2×4+2=10.'),('An undirected graph has 7 edges. How many neighbour entries are stored in ordinary adjacency lists?',14,'Store each edge at both endpoints.','2×7=14 entries.')])
L(4,'analysis','Asymptotic time, space and recurrences','Asymptotic worst case time;Space complexity;Big O;Theta;Omega','Loops, recursion and logarithms.',
'Asymptotic analysis studies how cost grows with input size. It abstracts constants but must still describe the operation being measured and the case: worst-case, average-case or amortized. A bound without those assumptions can mislead.',
'''Bounds and cases::f=O(g) means f is eventually at most a constant multiple of g; Ω is a lower bound and Θ is both. A worst-case time function maximizes over size-n inputs. Average-case needs an input distribution; amortized cost averages over an operation sequence without necessarily involving randomness. Best-case O(n) does not imply worst-case O(n).
||Loops and recursion::Consecutive loops add costs; nested loops sum inner counts. A triangular loop executes Σᵢ₌₁ⁿi=n(n+1)/2 operations. Halving a variable repeatedly yields Θ(log n) iterations. For T(n)=aT(n/b)+f(n), compare f with n^(log_b a) under the Master theorem’s applicable conditions; irregular recurrences may need a recursion tree or substitution.
||Space accounting::Report input storage and auxiliary storage separately. Recursive stack memory follows the maximum active depth and frame size. Sequential calls reuse stack space once previous calls return. An algorithm can run Θ(n log n) total time with only Θ(log n) recursive stack if frames use constant additional space and divide inputs without copying.''',
'''Analyse for i=1..n, for j=1..i, one operation::The inner count depends on i, so total is 1+2+…+n.~The sum is n(n+1)/2.~Leading growth is n², hence Θ(n²), not Θ(n) merely because each body is constant-time.
||Solve T(n)=2T(n/2)+n::Each recursion level performs total n combining work.~There are log₂n levels before constant-size leaves.~Total is Θ(n log n); a balanced recursion uses Θ(log n) stack depth with constant-size frames.''',
'Do not confuse maximum depth with total call count.||Big-O alone does not assert a tight bound.',
'Nested costs require summation.||Time counts all work; auxiliary space counts simultaneous storage.',
[('For n=4, how many operations are in Σᵢ₌₁ⁿ i?',10,'Use n(n+1)/2.','4×5/2=10.'),('Starting at 32, how many divisions by 2 reach 1?',5,'Count powers of two.','32→16→8→4→2→1: five divisions.')])
L(4,'search-sort','Searching and sorting with invariants','Searching;Sorting;Binary search;Merge sort;Quicksort;Heapsort','Arrays, asymptotic analysis and recursion.',
'A search or sort is easier to understand through its invariant: what remains true after every iteration or recursive step. Complexity then follows from how fast the unresolved region shrinks and how much work is done to combine results.',
'''Binary search::Maintain a range that could contain the key in sorted data. Compare its midpoint, discard the half ruled out by ordering, and update bounds so the loop progresses. After k comparisons the candidate range is about n/2ᵏ, producing Θ(log n) worst-case time. Unsorted input destroys the discard argument. Boundary variants such as first occurrence need their own invariant.
||Comparison sorts::Insertion sort maintains a sorted prefix and inserts each new key, giving Θ(n²) worst-case and Θ(n) best-case on already sorted input. Merge sort recursively sorts halves and linearly merges them, giving Θ(n log n) and ordinarily Θ(n) auxiliary array storage. Heapsort builds a heap then repeatedly extracts, giving Θ(n log n) worst-case and an in-place array implementation.
||Quicksort and lower bounds::Partition around a pivot, then recurse on both sides. Balanced partitions produce Θ(n log n); consistently extreme pivots produce Θ(n²). Randomization gives expected Θ(n log n), not a worst-case guarantee. General comparison sorting needs Ω(n log n) comparisons in the worst case; counting/radix sorts exploit additional assumptions and are not counterexamples.''',
'''Binary-search 7 in [1,3,5,7,9]::Midpoint index 2 contains 5; discard indices 0–2.~In remaining range 3–4, midpoint 3 contains 7.~Return index 3. The invariant held because the array was sorted.
||Merge [1,4,8] and [2,3,9]::Compare front elements and take 1, then 2, then 3.~Take 4, then 8; one input is exhausted.~Append remaining 9. Each element is moved once, so merging six elements is linear.''',
'Quicksort expected performance is not its worst case.||Binary search requires the monotonic ordering condition.',
'Merge/heap worst-case Θ(n log n); quicksort worst-case Θ(n²).||Comparison sorting lower bound Ω(n log n).',
[('What is the zero-based position of 8 in the sorted array [2,4,6,8,10]?',3,'Count indices from zero.','The positions are 0,1,2,3,4; 8 is at index 3.'),('Merging sorted lists of lengths 4 and 6 outputs how many elements?',10,'No elements are lost.','4+6=10 output elements.')])
L(4,'hashing','Hashing, collisions and probing','Hashing;Chaining;Open addressing;Load factor','Arrays, modular arithmetic and complexity.',
'A hash function chooses a bucket from a key. Different keys may choose the same bucket, so collision resolution is part of the data structure, not an exceptional error. Expected constant-time behaviour depends on hashing quality and load, not just using the word “hash”.',
'''Separate chaining::Each bucket holds a collection of colliding entries. With m buckets and n keys, load factor α=n/m is the average chain length. Under a uniform hashing assumption, searches take expected O(1+α). Chaining permits α>1, while long or adversarially concentrated chains can still produce O(n) worst-case search.
||Open addressing::All entries occupy slots in the array. Linear probing tries h(k),h(k)+1,… modulo m, causing primary clustering. Quadratic probing and double hashing use different probe sequences with coverage conditions. The table must have empty space for ordinary insertion; load factor approaches 1 with worsening probe costs.
||Deletion and invariants::An unsuccessful search can stop at a never-used empty slot. Deleting by making a slot simply empty can break a probe chain and hide later entries. Use a tombstone or a correct relocation strategy. Resizing rehashes existing keys because the mapping depends on the new capacity; copying old slot positions is not sufficient.''',
'''Insert 12,22,32 using h(k)=k mod 10 and linear probing::12 maps to slot 2 and occupies it.~22 also maps to 2, so it probes to 3.~32 probes occupied 2 and 3, landing at 4. All three are searchable only if the probe chain remains intact.
||Delete 22 from that table::Replacing slot 3 by a never-used empty marker would make a search for 32 stop too soon.~Mark slot 3 as a tombstone instead.~Search continues through the tombstone to slot 4, while a later insertion may reuse the tombstone.''',
'Expected O(1) is conditional on assumptions and load.||Deleting a probed entry requires preserving search paths.',
'Load factor α=n/m.||Chaining and open addressing have different capacity constraints.',
[('A hash table has 40 keys and 20 buckets. What is its load factor?',2,'Divide keys by buckets.','α=40/20=2; this is possible with chaining.'),('With h(k)=k mod 7, which initial slot is chosen for key 24?',3,'Find the remainder.','24=3×7+3, so slot 3.')])
L(4,'greedy-divide','Greedy and divide-and-conquer design','Greedy;Divide and conquer;Exchange argument','Recursion, sorting and asymptotic analysis.',
'Divide-and-conquer solves smaller problems and combines their answers. Greedy algorithms instead commit to a local choice without revisiting it. A plausible local rule is not enough: prove why an optimal solution can include that choice.',
'''Divide-and-conquer pattern::Define a base case, split into smaller subproblems, recursively solve, and combine. Merge sort uses two independent halves; binary search recurses into only one. Write a recurrence including the combine cost. Balanced splitting gives shallow recursion; an unbalanced split may erase the expected benefit.
||Greedy correctness::State the local choice and residual problem. An exchange argument takes an optimal solution and replaces part of it with the greedy choice without worsening its objective, then applies induction. In activity selection, choosing the earliest-finishing compatible activity leaves the largest opportunity for subsequent activities.
||Where greedy fails::Fractional knapsack sorts by value/weight ratio because fractional exchange is allowed. 0/1 knapsack forbids splitting, so the same strategy can miss a better combination. A counterexample is sufficient to disprove an unsupported greedy rule. MST algorithms work because cut/cycle properties justify their choices, not because every weighted optimization is greedy-solvable.''',
'''Select nonoverlapping activities [1,3],[2,5],[3,4],[4,7]::Sort by finish time: [1,3],[3,4],[2,5],[4,7].~Choose [1,3], then [3,4]; reject [2,5] because it starts before the current finish.~Choose [4,7], giving three compatible activities under the endpoint-sharing convention.
||Show ratio greedy fails for capacity 50 with (weight,value)=(10,60),(20,100),(30,120)::Ratios are 6,5,4, so greedy takes the first two for value 160 and leaves 20 capacity.~Items of weights 20 and 30 together fill capacity and give 220.~That better feasible solution disproves ratio greedy for 0/1 choices.''',
'A correct greedy algorithm needs a property or proof.||Fractional and indivisible choices change the optimization problem.',
'Divide: split, solve, combine.||Greedy: prove safe choice, then solve residual problem.',
[('In the capacity-50 counterexample, what is the optimal value?',220,'Compare feasible item pairs.','Weights 20+30=50 give values 100+120=220.'),('Under endpoint-sharing, how many activities are selected from [1,2],[2,3],[1,4] by earliest finish?',2,'Take the earliest finish, then the next compatible one.','Select [1,2] and [2,3], yielding 2.')])
L(4,'dynamic','Dynamic programming from state to solution','Dynamic programming;Optimal substructure;Memoization;Knapsack','Recursion, arrays and complexity.',
'Dynamic programming remembers answers to overlapping subproblems. Its difficult part is choosing what information a state must retain. Begin with a precise meaning for each table entry; a recurrence is valid only if it considers every allowed final choice.',
'''State and transition::Define state variables, then express an answer using smaller states. For 0/1 knapsack dp[i,w] is maximum value using the first i items within capacity w. Skipping item i gives dp[i−1,w]; including it, when feasible, gives valueᵢ+dp[i−1,w−weightᵢ]. The maximum covers both possibilities.
||Base cases and ordering::For knapsack, dp[0,w]=0 and dp[i,0]=0 when item weights are positive. Bottom-up order must make dependencies available; memoized recursion computes states on demand. A one-dimensional 0/1 knapsack update processes capacities downward so an item is not used multiple times in the same iteration. Upward updates correspond to a different reuse model.
||Cost and reconstruction::Complexity is number of reachable states times transition cost, not simply recursion depth. O(nW) knapsack depends on numeric capacity W and is pseudopolynomial in its binary encoding length. To recover selected items, record choices or compare table entries and walk backwards. Finding only the optimal value need not reveal a unique solution.''',
'''Knapsack capacity 4 with items (2,3),(3,4)::After the first item, capacities 2,3,4 can achieve value 3.~For the second item at capacity 4, skipping gives 3; including gives 4+dp[1,1]=4.~Optimal value is 4. Both items do not fit because their total weight is 5.
||Compute Fibonacci with bottom-up DP::Use F₀=0 and F₁=1.~Compute F₂=1,F₃=2,F₄=3,F₅=5 once each.~Only the last two values are needed for the next, so this sequence can use O(1) extra storage instead of a full table.''',
'A state must retain everything needed for future decisions.||Wrong iteration direction can silently turn 0/1 knapsack into unbounded reuse.',
'DP workflow: state, transition, base, order, complexity, reconstruction.||Overlapping subproblems make caching useful.',
[('With F₀=0,F₁=1,Fₙ=Fₙ₋₁+Fₙ₋₂, what is F₆?',8,'Build upward from the base cases.','0,1,1,2,3,5,8; F₆=8.'),('Capacity 3 allows items (weight,value)=(2,5),(3,7) once each. What is optimal value?',7,'Compare skip/include choices.','Both cannot fit. The second item alone gives 7, exceeding 5.')])
L(4,'graph-algorithms','Graph traversal, MSTs and shortest paths','Graph traversals;Minimum spanning trees;Shortest paths;BFS;DFS;Kruskal;Prim;Dijkstra;Bellman Ford','Graph representations, heaps and greedy proofs.',
'Graph traversal answers reachability; shortest paths minimize route cost; a minimum spanning tree minimizes the total cost of connecting every vertex. These objectives differ, so an MST path need not be a shortest path.',
'''BFS and DFS::BFS uses a queue and discovers vertices in layers, giving shortest edge-count paths in an unweighted graph. DFS uses recursion or a stack, finishing one branch before another. DFS supports cycle detection and topological order for directed acyclic graphs. Using adjacency lists, both inspect vertices and edges in O(V+E); mark vertices when enqueued to avoid duplicate expansion.
||MST algorithms::For a connected undirected weighted graph, a spanning tree has V−1 edges. Kruskal sorts edges by weight and uses disjoint sets to reject cycles. Prim grows one tree by repeatedly adding a cheapest edge leaving its current vertex set. The cut property justifies these choices. Disconnected input produces a minimum spanning forest rather than one spanning tree.
||Weighted shortest paths::Dijkstra finalizes the smallest tentative distance and requires nonnegative weights for that greedy argument. Bellman–Ford relaxes every edge repeatedly and detects a reachable negative cycle via further improvement after V−1 rounds. Floyd–Warshall permits intermediate vertices one by one and runs O(V³) for all pairs. Relaxation tests whether dist[u]+w(u,v) improves dist[v].''',
'''Triangle edges AB=1,BC=2,AC=5: find MST and A-to-C shortest path::Kruskal takes AB and BC, connecting all vertices for total 3.~The A-to-C path through B costs 1+2=3.~Direct AC costs 5, so the shortest path is 3. The objectives agree here, but that agreement is not a general theorem.
||One Dijkstra relaxation::Suppose dist[A]=4, dist[B]=10 and edge A→B has weight 3.~Candidate via A is 4+3=7.~Set dist[B]=7 and predecessor[B]=A; no update would occur if the candidate were at least 10.''',
'The usual Dijkstra correctness requires nonnegative weights.||An MST minimizes total tree weight, not each path.',
'BFS shortest paths require unweighted/unit edges.||MST edges=V−1.||Relaxation: candidate=dist[u]+w.',
[('A connected graph has 11 vertices. How many edges are in any spanning tree?',10,'A tree has one fewer edge than vertices.','11−1=10 edges.'),('dist[u]=6 and edge u→v has weight 4. What candidate distance to v is offered?',10,'Add the prefix path and new edge.','6+4=10.')])
